        fullContent: 'Persistence in assumption is not stubbornness or denial — it is the sustained occupation of a state of consciousness. When Neville says "I must dare to assume that I am what I want to be and I must continue in the assumption that I am until that which I have assumed is objectively realized," he is describing the most natural process in the world. The only thing unnatural about it is that most people persist in assumptions they do NOT want.\n\nWilliam Blake\'s aphorism "If the fool would persist in his folly, he would become wise" captures the paradox perfectly. What appears as foolish from the 3D perspective — assuming a state contradicted by all sensory evidence — is actually the highest wisdom when understood from the 4D perspective. The "fool" who persists in the assumption of health while the body shows symptoms, or wealth while the bank account shows lack, is practicing the very principle that creates worlds.\n\nPersistence does not mean repeating affirmations mechanically or forcing yourself to believe something you don\'t feel. It means returning to the feeling of the wish fulfilled again and again, each time deepening the conviction. It means refusing to accept the evidence of the senses as final. It means living FROM the end, not toward it.\n\nThe test of persistence comes when the 3D world seems to contradict your assumption most strongly. This is not a sign that the law has failed — it is the crucial moment when you must choose between the old state and the new. If you persist through this interval, the manifestation is guaranteed. If you waiver, you must begin again. The law does not punish your wavering — it simply responds to whatever assumption you are currently sustaining.',
